<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Mola Has Many Colors! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Beautiful Mola</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> A Mola Has Many Colors!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Express opinions about a familiar topic. interactive: Sustain conversations on a variety of topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express an opinion about a mola design (e.g., 'I think this mola is beautiful.'). Can sustain a discussion on mola designs in collaboration with peers, using reasons and follow-up questions (e.g., 'I like this design because it is colorful. Do you like it too?'). Can initiate a conversation about mola colors and designs and ask questions (e.g., 'What colors do you see?' 'I see red and blue.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b> This stage activates prior knowledge, introduces the topic, and models target language for speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of traditional molas with different colors and designs (e.g., a turtle mola, a butterfly mola, a jaguar mola). The teacher asks students, "What do you see?" and "What colors do you see?" encouraging short answers like "A mola!" "Red and blue!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a mola and says, "This is a mola. It has many colors. I like this mola because it is colorful." The teacher then points to another mola and says, "This mola has a turtle design. I think it is beautiful because it is unique." The teacher emphasizes the /m/ sound in 'mola' and the /k/ sound in 'colors' and 'creative'. The teacher models sentence structures for expressing opinions and reasons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'because' used to say 'why'?" (Expected answer: Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'colorful' mean it has one color or many colors?" (Expected answer: Many colors)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we say, 'I like this mola'?" (Expected answer: Yes)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Beautiful Mola")</b> Students are exposed to target language in context to understand its meaning and use.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two friends, Ana and Leo, discussing molas. The dialogue incorporates target vocabulary (mola, vibrant, unique, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, decorate, creative) and grammar ('because', 'A mola has...'). Visual aids showing the molas discussed in the dialogue will be displayed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Express opinions about a familiar topic and sustain conversations on a variety of topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, demonstrating clear pronunciation (/m/, /k/) and intonation for expressing preferences. Students listen and follow along with the visuals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are Ana and Leo talking about?" (Molás). "Do they like the molas?" (Yes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False questions:</b> "Ana likes the red mola. True or False?" "Leo likes the mola because it has a butterfly. True or False?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher also highlights sequence words used in the dialogue, such as "First, you choose the colors..." or "Next, you sew carefully."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</b> This stage provides controlled and semi-controlled practice to build accuracy in using the target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice expressing preferences and reasons using 'I like this because...', 'A mola has...', and instructional steps. Vocabulary: mola, creativity, project, inspiration, culture, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, cut, sew, paste, choose, express, illustrate, decorate, collaborate, turquoise, golden, round, vibrant, traditional, unique, carefully, skillfully, artistically, also, and, because, first, second, third, next, last. Pronunciation: /m/ in 'mola', /k/ in 'colors', spelling 'red,' 'orange,' 'vibrant,' 'second.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Builder:</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board: "I like this mola because it has _______ colors (e.g., vibrant/turquoise/golden)." or "A mola has many _______ (e.g., shapes/designs)." Students complete the sentences orally, individually or in chorus, using new adjectives and nouns. The teacher ensures correct pronunciation and spelling of words like 'vibrant' and 'second'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Pair Sentence Frames:</b> In pairs, students receive flashcards of different mola designs (turtle, butterfly, jaguar) and colors. They take turns using sentence frames to describe and give reasons: "I like this _______ mola because it is _______. Do you like it too?" "This mola has _______ colors and it is _______. What do you think?" The teacher circulates, providing feedback and prompting for more detail, ensuring they use 'and' and 'because' correctly. Students are encouraged to spell out colors when asking about them (e.g., "What colors do you see? R-E-D and B-L-U-E?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction - "Mola Talk":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair is given a set of three different mola pictures. One student picks a mola and describes it, expressing an opinion and giving a reason (e.g., "I think this mola is very creative because it has a big jaguar. It also has golden and turquoise colors."). The partner then asks a follow-up question (e.g., "What colors do you see?" or "Do you like the jaguar design?"). They switch roles. The teacher reminds them to acknowledge peers' contributions (e.g., "Your mola is very creative!").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Beautiful Mola")</b> Students use the language to complete a real-world task, focusing on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Mola Gallery Walk and Talk" - a collaborative discussion activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student brings a drawing of a mola they created (from a previous art class or as homework), or they are given a blank mola template to quickly decorate with crayons/markers. They are encouraged to make it vibrant and unique.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students stand around the classroom, displaying their mola drawings. This creates a "gallery."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups (3-4 students), they walk around the "gallery." When they stop at a mola, the creator describes their mola, expresses an opinion, and gives a reason. For example: "This is my mola. I chose red and yellow colors. I think it is beautiful because it has a round butterfly design. First, I decorated the butterfly, then I added many small shapes. What colors do you see?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students in the group listen, ask follow-up questions (e.g., "Do you like the red color?" "Why did you choose a butterfly?"), and share their own feedback (e.g., "I like your color choices!," "Your mola is very creative!"). They should use "and" and "also" to add information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The groups rotate to see all molas, sustaining conversation at each stop. The teacher facilitates, ensures everyone speaks, and encourages the use of target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>(Formative assessment strategy and Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket)</b> This stage evaluates students' ability to use the language for communicative purposes and allows for self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist based on specific speaking criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "Mola Gallery Walk and Talk," the teacher observes each student's participation. Specifically, the teacher assesses students' ability to describe their mola, express an opinion using 'I like this because...', and ask at least one question to a peer. Students will also be assessed on their ability to respond to questions and acknowledge peer contributions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /m/ in 'mola' and /k/ in 'colors/creative'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (mola, vibrant, unique, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, decorate, express, collaborate).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses 'because' to provide reasons for opinions/preferences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks relevant questions about molas ('What colors do you see?' 'Do you like it?').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sustains a short conversation (e.g., asks a question and responds to one).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Acknowledges peers' contributions and gives feedback (e.g., 'Your mola is very creative!').</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences orally before leaving class:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can describe a mola and say why I like it by saying, 'I like this mola because it is _______.'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask my friend about their mola by saying, 'What colors do you see?' or 'Do you like it too?'"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>(Student and Teacher Reflection; Connection to next lesson)</b> This stage encourages critical thinking about the learning process and informs future instruction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases did you use today to talk about molas?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you feel talking about your mola with your friends? Was it easy or difficult to express your ideas?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., "The gallery walk fostered real interaction. Students used 'because' effectively.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Some students still need more practice with asking follow-up questions. I could provide more visual prompts for sentence starters during peer discussions. Ensure they also use instructional steps clearly.")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), students will transition from orally expressing opinions and descriptions to writing short paragraphs about mola designs, incorporating their preferences and reasons, and describing basic steps of mola creation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>